   "root": "El 2, Elk, Eln",
   "verdict": "confirmed",
   "modern_form": "PIE *el- / *h₁el- 'red, brown' (in tree and animal names) — Pokorny '1. el-, ol-, l-' pp. 302-304; Watkins/AHD 'el-²'",
   "note": "The root box is right on all three counts. Pokorny's lemma reads 'Root/lemma: el-1, ol-, l- ... Meaning: red, brown (in names of trees and animals) ... Page(s): 302-304', so Nourai's gloss is a near-verbatim rendering of Pokorny's own and POK:302 is the correct page. His two extra head-names are Pokorny's own extensions: 'Elk' = the k-extension *olki- (OHG elaho, OE eolh, ON elgr 'elk'), 'Eln' = the n-stem *el-en-/*l-on- (Gk. ἔλαφος, Lith. elnis, Arm. eḷn 'deer'). KLN:509 is right too: Klein's printed p.509 (the leaf running from 'elevation' to 'ellipse' in KLN_1966.txt, between the page markers 508 and 510) carries s.v. elk 'These words derive fr. I.-E. base *elk-, a -k-enlargement of base *el-, ‚brown‘'. Watkins's appendix has 'el-2. Red, brown (forming animal and tree names)' and lists under it exactly ELM, ALDER, ELK, i.e. Nourai's nodes #4-#5, with '[Pok. 1. el- 302.]'. Two branches of the chart are weaker than the root: the Germanic lamb branch (nodes #6-#7) is Pokorny's and Klein's view but is called doubtful by Buck and Etymonline, and the Persian outcome آل (node #3) cannot be got out of Pahlavi arus by any Persian sound law — that link is the one real error on this chart.",
   "note_fa": "خودِ ریشه درست است: پوکورنی در صفحات ۳۰۲–۳۰۴ مادهٔ el-1 را با معنای «سرخ، قهوه‌ای — در نام درختان و جانوران» آورده و همان را نورایی ترجمه کرده است؛ دو نام دیگرِ سربرگ (Elk و Eln) نیز همان گسترش‌های *olki- و *el-en- در کتاب پوکورنی‌اند. کلاین نیز در ص ۵۰۹ زیر elk همین ریشه را می‌دهد و واتکینز در پیوستِ فرهنگ American Heritage زیر el-2 دقیقاً همین سه واژهٔ elm و alder و elk را می‌آورد. دو شاخه سست‌تر است: پیوندِ lamb به این ریشه را باک و اتیمولاین مشکوک می‌دانند، و مهم‌تر اینکه واژهٔ فارسی «آل» با هیچ دگرگونی آوایی شناخته‌ای از پهلوی arus به دست نمی‌آید و تنها خطای جدی این نمودار همان پیکان است.",
   "sources": [E_ELK],
   "ref_check": [
    {"ref": "POK:302", "status": "supports", "note": "POK_full.txt block 'Root /lemma: el-1, ol- el- ... Meaning: red, brown (in names of trees and animals) ... Page(s): 302-304' (lines 25153-25351). Its material section A opens 'Old Indic arusa-h ‚fire-color‘, Avestan aurusa- ‚white‘; Germanic *elwa- ‚brown, yellow‘ in Old High German elo' and section B/C give the alder, elm and elk words — the whole chart except the Persian outcome."},
    {"ref": "KLN:509", "status": "supports", "note": "KLN_1966.txt, printed page 509 (between the OCR page markers '508' at line 12823 and '510' at line 12851), s.v. elk: 'These words derive fr. I.-E. base *elk-, a -k-enlargement of base *el-, ‚brown‘. For derivatives of *eln-, -n-enlargement of base *-el-, see eland'. The same printed page also carries 'ell', which is why Nourai cites p.509 for the next root as well."}
   ],
   "consulted": [
    {"src": "AHD", "where": "Watkins 1985, s.v. el-2 (AHD_watkins1985.txt line 435)", "stance": "supports", "note": "'el-2. Red, brown (forming animal and tree names). 1. Extended form *elmo- in Germanic *elmo-, *almo- in Old English elm, elm: ELM. 2. Germanic extended form *aliza, alder, in Old English alor: ALDER. ... 4. Extended o-grade form *olki- in Germanic *alkiz, elk ... : ELK. [Pok. 1. el- 302.]'"},
    {"src": "WLD", "where": "Bd. I 151-159 (WLD_Bd1_pages.txt leaves 162-167)", "stance": "supports", "note": "Walde-Pokorny treat the same complex: p.154 the stem '*el-en-, *el-n-, mit Schwundstufe der Wz. *l-on-' with Gk. ἔλαφος and Goth. lamb, p.159 'Verglichen wird ai. aruna- ‚rötlich, goldgelb‘, arusá- ‚rot, feuerfarben‘ ..., av. aurusa- ‚weiß‘ (vgl. S. 359 unter reudh-), wohl mit Recht'."},
    {"src": "SYN", "where": "Buck 3.29 'Lamb', p.160", "stance": "partial", "note": "'Goth., ON, OE lamb, etc., the general Gmc. word, perh.: Grk. ἔλαφος ‚stag‘ and other animal names, but root connection and primary sense dub. Walde-P. 1.154.' — Buck accepts the root but flags the lamb branch as doubtful."}
   ],
   "nodes": [
    {
     "id": 1, "lang": "Avestan", "words": "auruša", "verdict": "confirmed",
     "derivation": "Avestan auruša- 'white' is the exact Iranian counterpart of Sanskrit aruṣá- 'reddish, flame-coloured' (epithet of Agni's horses); both continue Indo-Iranian *arušá-, a u-stem colour adjective built on PIE *el- 'red, brown'. The Avestan au- for expected a- is the regular Avestan u-epenthesis in the sequence a-ru- (compare Av. auruna- 'wild' beside Skt. aruṇá-), not a separate ablaut grade. In the Yašts it is an animal colour word — aspahe kəhrpa aurušahe 'in the shape of a white horse' (Yt. 8.18-21), pasūm aurušəm 'a white sheep' (Yt. 8.58) — and it is the Iranian narrowing 'ruddy' > 'bright pale' > 'white' that produces Nourai's double gloss 'pale red, white'.",
     "derivation_fa": "اوستایی auruša- «سپید» برابرِ دقیقِ سنسکریت aruṣá- «سرخ‌فام، آتش‌رنگ» است و هر دو از هندوایرانی *arušá- می‌آیند، ستاکی بر ریشهٔ هندواروپایی *el- «سرخ، قهوه‌ای». الفِ آغازین در اوستایی به سبب «پیش‌آوایی» (epenthesis) پیش از ru به au- بدل شده است، همان‌گونه که auruna- در برابرِ aruṇá-ی سنسکریت. بارتولومه (ستون ۱۹۰) آن را صفتِ جانوران می‌داند: «اسبِ سپید»، «گوسفندِ سپید». تحول معنایی «سرخ‌فام > روشن > سپید» در ایرانی رخ داده است؛ ادعای نورایی درست است.",
     "sources": [],
     "ref_check": [
      {"ref": "POK:302", "status": "supports", "note": "Pokorny el-1 (pp.302-304), material A: 'Old Indie arusa-h ‚fire-color‘, Avestan aurusa- ‚white‘; Germanic *elwa- ‚brown, yellow‘' (POK_full.txt line ~25163) — exactly the arrow drawn."}
     ],
     "consulted": [
      {"src": "BRT", "where": "col. 190 (BRT.txt line 5386, running head 'aurusa- / 190')", "stance": "supports", "note": "'j. auruša- Adj. ‚weiss‘, 1) von Tieren: pasūm .. ˚həm ... ‚ein weisses oder schwarzes Schaf‘ Yt.8.58; — aspahe kəhrpa ˚he .. ‚in Gestalt eines weissen Rosses‘ Yt.8.18-21'. This is the column Mo'in's Borhan footnote cites for the whole chain."},
      {"src": "WLD", "where": "Bd. I 159 (WLD_Bd1_pages.txt line 14651)", "stance": "supports", "note": "'Verglichen wird ai. aruna- ‚rötlich, goldgelb‘, arusá- ‚rot, feuerfarben‘ (Fick III⁴ 27), av. aurusa- ‚weiß‘ (vgl. S. 359 unter reudh-), wohl mit Recht' — Walde-Pokorny accept the equation but note the competing tie to *reudh- 'red', which matters for node #3."}
     ]
    },
    {
